" (https://personalpages.manchester.ac.uk/staff/marcus.webb/pdfs/webbAZpaper.pdf)\nciteturn242view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 23; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://personalpages.manchester.ac.uk/staff/marcus.webb/pdfs/webbAZpaper.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1806\nL0@P0: THE AZ ALGORITHM FOR LEAST SQUARES SYSTEMS WITH\nL1@P0: A KNOWN INCOMPLETE GENERALIZED INVERSE\nL2@P0: VINCENT COPPE´∗, DAAN HUYBRECHS∗, ROEL MATTHYSEN∗, AND MARCUS WEBB†\nL3@P0: Abstract. We introduce an algorithm for the least squares solution of a rectangular linear system\nL4@P0: Ax = b, in which A may be arbitrarily ill-conditioned. We assume that a complementary matrix Z\nL5@P0: is known such that A − AZ∗A is numerically low rank. Loosely speaking, Z\nL6@P0: ∗ acts like a generalized\nL7@P0: inverse of A up to a numerically low rank error. We give several examples of (A, Z) combinations\nL8@P0: in function approximation, where we can achieve high-order approximations in a number of non\u0002standard settings: the approximation of functions on domains with irregular shapes, weighted least\nL9@P0: squares problems with highly skewed weights, and the spectral approximation of functions with\nL10@P0: localized singularities. The algorithm is most efficient when A and Z\nL11@P0: ∗ have fast matrix-vector\nL12@P0: multiplication and when the numerical rank of A − AZ∗A is small.\nL13@P0: Key words. Least squares problems, ill-conditioned systems, randomized linear algebra, frames,\nL14@P0: low rank matrix approximation\nL15@P0: AMS subject classifications. [MSC2010] Primary: 65F20, Secondary: 65F05, 68W20\nL16@P0: 1. Introduction. The topic of this paper arose from the study of function ap\u0002proximation using frames [2, 1]. Frames are sets of functions that, unlike a basis,\nL17@P0: may be redundant. This leads to improved flexibility in a variety of settings which we\nL18@P0: illustrate below, but it also comes at a cost: the approximation problem is equivalent\nL19@P0: to solving a linear system Ax = b that is, owing to the redundancy of the frame,\nL20@P0: ill-conditioned. The central result of [2, 1] is that the function at hand can neverthe\u0002less be approximated to high accuracy via a regularized least squares approximation.\nL21@P0: Thus, the linear system is rectangular, A ∈ C\nL22@P0: M×N with M > N, and the cost of a\nL23@P0: direct solver scales as O(MN2), where typically M is at least linear in N.\nL24@P0: The particular type of frame that has received the most attention in this context\nL25@P0: corresponds to Fourier extension or Fourier continuation [3, 4, 12], where a smooth\nL26@P0: but non-periodic function on a domain Ω is approximated by a Fourier series on\nL27@P0: a larger bounding box. Here, one can think of the redundancy as corresponding\nL28@P0: to different extensions of f from Ω to the bounding box. Fast algorithms for the\nL29@P0: construction of Fourier extension approximations were proposed by Lyon in [14] for\nL30@P0: univariate problems and by Matthysen and Huybrechs in [15, 16] for more general\nL31@P0: problems in one and two dimensions. These algorithms have the form of the proposed\nL32@P0: AZ-algorithm in the special case where Z = A.\nL33@P0: Thus, the AZ algorithm is a generalization of the algorithms proposed for Fourier\nL34@P0: extension in [14, 15, 16]. In this paper we analyze the algorithm from the point of view\nL35@P0: of linear algebra, rather than approximation theory. We supply error estimates when\nL36@P0: using techniques from randomized linear algebra for the first step of the AZ algorithm\nL37@P0: [11]. In contrast, the previous analysis in [15, 16] has focused exclusively on studying\nL38@P0: the numerical rank of this system, in the specific setting of Fourier extension problems.\nL39@P0: We characterize the scope of the algorithm and provide several examples in different\nL40@P0: settings. In our analysis we focus on the residual of the linear system, as its size is\nL41@P0: directly related to the approximation error in the examples given.\nL42@P0: ∗Department of Computer Science, KU Leuven, Belgium. vincent.coppe@cs.kuleuven.be,\nL43@P0: daan.huybrechs@cs.kuleuven.be, roelmatthysen@gmail.com\nL44@P0: †Department of Mathematics, University of Manchester, UK. marcus.webb@manchester.ac.uk.\nL45@P0: This author is grateful to FWO Research Foundation Flanders for the postdoctoral fellowship he\nL46@P0: enjoyed during the research for this paper.\nL47@P0-1: 12 V. COPPE, D. HUYBRECHS, R. MATTHYSEN, M. WEBB ´\nL48@P1: There is a rich body of literature in numerical linear algebra on solution methods\nL49@P1: for least squares problems. We refer the reader to the standard references [10, 13].\nL50@P1: We note that direct solvers typically exhibit cubic complexity in the dimension of the\nL51@P1: problem: the aim of the AZ algorithm is to reduce that complexity by exploiting the\nL52@P1: specific structure of certain least squares problems. This leads to an algorithm with\nL53@P1: several steps, in which existing methods for least squares problems can be used for\nL54@P1: systems with lower rank. In particular we study SVD and QR based methods for step\nL55@P1: 1 of the algorithm. We briefly comment on the applicability of iterative solvers for\nL56@P1: least squares problems, such as LSQR [17] and LSMR [9], near the end of the paper.\nL57@P1: The structure of the paper is as follows. We formulate the algorithm in §2 and\nL58@P1: state some general algebraic properties. We analyze the residual of solvers for low-rank\nL59@P1: systems based on randomized SVD or randomized QR in §3. After the analysis, we\nL60@P1: illustrate the algorithms with a sequence of examples: Fourier extension approxima\u0002tion in §4, approximation using weighted bases in §5, spline-based extension problems\nL61@P1: in §6 and weighted least squares problems in §7. A Jupyter notebook containing the\nL62@P1: Julia code of these examples is found in [7]. We end the paper with some concluding\nL63@P1: remarks in §8.\nL64@P1: 2. The AZ algorithm. Given a linear system Ax = b, with A ∈ C\nL65@P1: M×N , and an\nL66@P1: additional matrix Z ∈ C\nL67@P1: M×N , the AZ algorithm consists of three steps.\nL68@P1: Algorithm 2.1 The AZ algorithm\nL69@P1: Input: A, Z ∈ C\nL70@P1: M×N , b ∈ CM\nL71@P1: Output: x ∈ C\nL72@P1: N such that Ax ≈ b in a least squares sense\nL73@P1: 1: Solve (I − AZ∗)Ax1 = (I − AZ∗)b\nL74@P1: 2: x2 ← Z\nL75@P1: ∗\nL76@P1: (b − Ax1)\nL77@P1: 3: x ← x1 + x2\nL78@P1: It is stated that the output solves Ax ≈ b in a least squares sense, though the\nL79@P1: algorithm does not specify which matrix Z to use, nor which solver to use in step\nL80@P1: 1, and of course the properties of the output depend on that. We will give precise\nL81@P1: statements in the analysis that follows for particular choices. Overall, the intention\nL82@P1: is to choose Z such that the matrix (I − AZ∗)A approximately has low rank. Since\nL83@P1: (I − AZ∗)A = A − AZ∗A, this property can be thought of as Z\nL84@P1: ∗ being an incomplete\nL85@P1: generalized inverse of A. We analyze a few choices of solvers in §3, and we remark\nL86@P1: on the choice of Z in later sections. Remarkably, the following two statements are\nL87@P1: independent of these choices.\nL88@P1: Lemma 2.1 (AZ Lemma). Let xˆ = xˆ1 + xˆ2 be output from the AZ algorithm.\nL89@P1: Then the final residual is equal to the residual of step 1.\nL90@P1: Proof. A simple expansion of the final residual yields\nL91@P1: b − Axˆ = b − Axˆ1 − Axˆ2\nL92@P1: = b − Axˆ1 − AZ∗(b − Axˆ1)\nL93@P1: = (I − AZ∗)(b − Axˆ1).\nL94@P1: This is precisely the residual from step 1 of the algorithm.\nL95@P1: Thus, the accuracy with which step 1 is solved determines the accuracy of the\nL96@P1-2: overall algorithm, at least when accuracy is measured in terms of the size of theTHE AZ ALGORITHM 3\nL97@P2: residual. Furthermore, the computational cost of the algorithm is also shifted to step\nL98@P2: 1, because step 2 and 3 only involve matrix-vector multiplication and addition.\nL99@P2: In the following statement, we use the terminology of a stable least squares fit.\nL100@P2: A stable least squares fit corresponds to a solution vector that has itself a moderate\nL101@P2: norm and that yields a small residual. The relevance of a stable least squares fit in the\nL102@P2: setting of our examples later in this paper is that, if a stable least squares fit exists,\nL103@P2: then numerical methods exist that can reliably find such a solution, no matter how\nL104@P2: ill-conditioned the linear system is.\nL105@P2: One way to compute a stable least squares fit is based on a regularized SVD and\nL106@P2: this case was studied for function approximation using frames in [2]. The following\nL107@P2: lemma proves that the AZ algorithm can also, in principle, find a stable least squares\nL108@P2: fit (if it exists).\nL109@P2: Lemma 2.2 (Stable least squares fitting). Let A ∈ C\nL110@P2: M×N , b ∈ CM, and suppose\nL111@P2: there exists x˜ ∈ C\nL112@P2: N such that\nL113@P2: (2.1) ∥b − Ax˜∥2 ≤ τ, ∥x˜∥2 ≤ C,\nL114@P2: for τ, C > 0. Then there exists a solution xˆ1 to step 1 of the AZ algorithm such that\nL115@P2: the residual of the computed vector xˆ = xˆ1 + xˆ2 satisfies,\nL116@P2: (2.2) ∥b − Axˆ∥2 ≤ ∥I − AZ∗∥2τ, ∥xˆ∥2 ≤ C + ∥Z\nL117@P2: ∗\nL118@P2: ∥2τ\nL119@P2: Proof. Take ˆx1 = x˜. Then Lemma 2.1 implies b − Axˆ = (I − AZ∗)(b − Ax˜), which\nL120@P2: gives us the first inequality. The second inequality follows from ˆx = x˜ + Z\nL121@P2: ∗\nL122@P2: (b − Ax˜).\nL123@P2: Expression (2.2) hints at the expected algebraic regime in which the AZ algorithm\nL124@P2: may work well, namely when both A and Z\nL125@P2: ∗\nL126@P2: (and therefore I − AZ∗as well) have\nL127@P2: moderately bounded norms. In that case, the lemma shows that a stable least squares\nL128@P2: fit can be recovered with norm and residual that are comparable to any other stable\nL129@P2: fit. This is also the regime in which the notion of a stable least squares fit itself is\nL130@P2: relevant. Indeed, if A is ill-conditioned but bounded, then it must have small singular\nL131@P2: values or, equivalently, a non-trivial (numerical) null space. The consequences are\nL132@P2: two-fold: there may be multiple different solutions with comparable residual, i.e., a\nL133@P2: solution vector may not be unique, and there is a risk of returning a large solution\nL134@P2: vector x, which is often undesirable. The aim in the choice of the solver in step 1 and\nL135@P2: the choice of Z is to compute a stable least squares fit.\nL136@P2: 2.1. Choosing the matrix Z. In principle, the AZ algorithm can be used with\nL137@P2: any matrix Z. However, we must choose Z wisely if we want efficiency and accuracy.\nL138@P2: If the norm of Z\nL139@P2: ∗\nL140@P2: is large then Lemma 2.2 suggests that we may fail to obtain a\nL141@P2: stable least squares fit. If A − AZ∗A is not low rank then the cost of solving the least\nL142@P2: squares system in step 1 may be expensive. Ideally, both A and Z\nL143@P2: ∗\nL144@P2: also have fast\nL145@P2: matrix-vector multiplication.\nL146@P2: Two extreme choices are Z = 0 and Z = (A\nL147@P2: †\nL148@P2: )\nL149@P2: ∗\nL150@P2: . In the case Z = 0, step 1 solves\nL151@P2: the original system and step 2 returns zero. We do not gain any efficiency over simply\nL152@P2: solving the original system. In the latter case, it is exactly the opposite: if Z = (A\nL153@P2: †\nL154@P2: )\nL155@P2: ∗\nL156@P2: ,\nL157@P2: then the first step yields x1 = 0 and the original system is solved in the second step\nL158@P2: using the pseudoinverse of A. This may be efficient, but we would need to know the\nL159@P2: pseudoinverse of A! In addition, the solution vector may be very large.\nL160@P2: Any other choice of Z leads to a situation in between: part of the solution is\nL161@P2: found using a direct solver in step 1, part of it is found by multiplication with Z\nL162@P2: ∗\nL163@P2-3: in4 V. COPPE, D. HUYBRECHS, R. MATTHYSEN, M. WEBB ´\nL164@P3: step 2. We desire a matrix Z such that A − AZ∗A has low numerical rank, so that\nL165@P3: step 1 can be solved efficiently, and Z\nL166@P3: ∗ must be readily available with an efficient\nL167@P3: matrix-vector multiply if possible. Much like a preconditioner, we choose Z satisfying\nL168@P3: these properties using some a priori information about the underlying problem.\nL169@P3: The following lemma gives a general relationship between A and Z which would\nL170@P3: guarantee A − AZ∗A to be numerically low rank.\nL171@P3: Lemma 2.3. Suppose that A, Z ∈ C\nL172@P3: M×N satisfy\nL173@P3: A = W + L1 + E1, Z∗= W† + L2 + E2,\nL174@P3: where rank(L1),rank(L2) ≤ R and ∥E1∥F, ∥E2∥F ≤ ε. Here W†is the Moore-Penrose\nL175@P3: pseudoinverse. Then\nL176@P3: A − AZ∗A = L + E,\nL177@P3: where rank(L) ≤ 3R and\nL178@P3: (2.3) ∥E∥F ≤ ε (1 + ∥I − AZ∗∥2 + ∥A∥\nL179@P3: 2\nL180@P3: 2\nL181@P3: ) + ε\nL182@P3: 2\nL183@P3: ∥A∥2.\nL184@P3: The result is exactly the same if the norms on E1, E2 and E are changed to ∥ ⋅ ∥2.\nL185@P3: Proof. We simply expand A − AZ∗A. For clarity we expand terms gradually.\nL186@P3: A − AZ∗A = W + L1 + E1 − AZ∗W − AZ∗(L1 + E1)\nL187@P3: = W + L1 + E1 − AW†W − A(L2 + E2)W − AZ∗(L1 + E1)\nL188@P3: = W + L1 + E1 − WW†W − (L1 + E1)W†W\nL189@P3: − A(L2 + E2)W − AZ∗(L1 + E1)\nL190@P3: Since W†is a generalized inverse of W, we have W − WW†W = 0, so\nL191@P3: A − AZ∗A = L1 + E1 − (L1 + E1)W†W − A(L2 + E2)W − AZ∗(L1 + E1).\nL192@P3: Now, writing A(L2 + E2)W = AL2W + AE2A − AE2L1 − AE2E1 and splitting up the\nL193@P3: low rank and small norm parts, we obtain,\nL194@P3: A − AZ∗A = (I − AZ∗ + AE2)L1 − L1W†W − AL2W\nL195@P3: ´¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¸¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¶\nL196@P3: L\nL197@P3: + (I − AZ∗)E1 − E1WW† − AE2A + AE2E1\nL198@P3: ´¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¸ ¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¹¶\nL199@P3: E\nL200@P3: It is clear that rank(L) ≤ 3R because of the three occurrences of L1 and L2. The\nL201@P3: Frobenius norm of E is bounded above by\nL202@P3: ∥E∥F ≤ ∥(I − AZ∗)E1∥F + ∥WW†E1∥F + ∥AE2A∥F + ∥AE2E1∥F\nL203@P3: ≤ ∥E1∥F∥I − AZ∗∥2 + ∥WW†∥2∥E1∥F + ∥E2∥F∥A∥\nL204@P3: 2\nL205@P3: 2 + ∥E1∥F ∥E2∥2∥A∥2\nL206@P3: Here we have used the inequality ∥BC∥F ≤ ∥B∥2∥C∥F for general rectangular matrices\nL207@P3: B and C\nL208@P3: 1\nL209@P3: .\nL210@P3: 1The proof of this is a one-liner: ∥BC∥2\nL211@P3: F\nL212@P3: = ∑j∥Bcj ∥\nL213@P3: 2\nL214@P3: 2\nL215@P3: ≤ ∥B∥\nL216@P3: 2\nL217@P3: 2 ∑j\nL218@P3: ∥cj ∥\nL219@P3: 2\nL220@P3: 2\nL221@P3: = ∥B∥\nL222@P3: 2\nL223@P3: 2\nL224@P3: ∥C∥\nL225@P3: 2\nL226@P3-4: FTHE AZ ALGORITHM 5\nL227@P4: Because W†is the Moore-Penrose pseudoinverse of W, the matrix WW†is an\nL228@P4: orthogonal projection onto the image of W, so ∥WW†∥2 ≤ 1. Furthermore, ∥E2∥2 ≤\nL229@P4: ∥E2∥F ≤ ε, which enables us to arrive at the desired bound on ∥E∥F in equation (2.3).\nL230@P4: In order to prove the same result with the Frobenius norms on E1, E2 and E\nL231@P4: replaced by ∥⋅∥2, it is readily checked that the exact same proof holds with all instances\nL232@P4: of F replaced by 2. In particular, we have the inequality ∥BC∥2 ≤ ∥B∥2∥C∥2.\nL233@P4: This lemma focuses on the rank of A−AZ∗A. Following Lemma 2.2, and justified\nL234@P4: further by (2.3), we also desire A and Z\nL235@P4: ∗\nL236@P4: to be bounded, even if A\nL237@P4: †\nL238@P4: is not. In the\nL239@P4: decomposition of this lemma, this implies that both W and its pseudoinverse W†\nL240@P4: should be bounded, but L\nL241@P4: †\nL242@P4: 1\nL243@P4: need not be. In other words, if A is seen as consisting of\nL244@P4: a well-conditioned part and an ill-conditioned part, then Z\nL245@P4: ∗\nL246@P4: acts like an approximate\nL247@P4: generalized inverse of the well-conditioned part.\nL248@P4: In our examples, A and Z are determined by analytical means, which are appli\u0002cation-specific. We do not explicitly compute the W and L1, L2 matrices of the above\nL249@P4: lemma. The effectiveness of the algorithm merely relies on the fact that these matrices\nL250@P4: exist.\nL251@P4: 3. Fast randomized algorithms for numerically low-rank systems. In\nL252@P4: this section we discuss fast algorithms for the solution of the system Ax = b, where\nL253@P4: A ∈ C\nL254@P4: M×N , with M ≥ N, has epsilon rank r. We write rankε(A) = r and in this paper\nL255@P4: it means that there exists L, E ∈ C\nL256@P4: M×N such that\nL257@P4: (3.1) A = L + E, where rank(L) = r and ∥E∥F ≤ ε.\nL258@P4: It is important to note that we have used the Frobenius norm here, which implies\nL259@P4: ∑k>r σ\nL260@P4: 2\nL261@P4: k\nL262@P4: ≤ ε\nL263@P4: 2\nL264@P4: , where σr+1, . . . , σN are the N − r smallest singular values of A. This is\nL265@P4: necessary for our proofs of the error bounds. Throughout this section there are points\nL266@P4: where the Frobenius norm appears to have been used unnecessarily where the 2-norm\nL267@P4: could have been used, but in all cases we do not believe that the final bounds will\nL268@P4: be improved significantly by changing to the 2-norm. This is due to the fact that we\nL269@P4: are not aware of an effective version of Proposition 3.1 which bounds 2-norms of the\nL270@P4: relevant random matrices purely in terms of 2-norms of other matrices.\nL271@P4: We make use of Gaussian random matrices, Ω ∼ N (0, 1; R\nL272@P4: n×k\nL273@P4: ), which are n × k\nL274@P4: matrices whose elements are independent Gaussian random variables with mean 0\nL275@P4: and variance 1.\nL276@P4: Proposition 3.1 ([11]). Let Ω ∼ N (0, 1; R\nL277@P4: n×k\nL278@P4: ), and let S, T ∈ R\nL279@P4: n×k\nL280@P4: be fixed\nL281@P4: matrices. Then for all u ≥ 0,\nL282@P4: E{∥S\nL283@P4: ∗ΩT∥F} ≤ ∥S∥F∥T∥F, P{∥S∗ΩT∥F ≥ (1 + u) ⋅ ∥S∥F∥T∥F} ≤ e−\nL284@P4: u\nL285@P4: 2\nL286@P4: 2\nL287@P4: Proposition 3.2 ([11]). Let Ω ∼ N (0, 1; R\nL288@P4: r×(r+p)\nL289@P4: ) with p ≥ 4. Then for all s ≥ 1,\nL290@P4: E{∥Ω\nL291@P4: †\nL292@P4: ∥F} =\nL293@P4: √ r\nL294@P4: p − 1\nL295@P4: , P{∥Ω\nL296@P4: †\nL297@P4: ∥F ≥ s ⋅\nL298@P4: √ 3r\nL299@P4: p + 1\nL300@P4: } ≤ s\nL301@P4: −p\nL302@P4: Note that we intend to apply the results of this section to step 1 of the AZ\nL303@P4: algorithm. Thus, matrix A in this section actually corresponds to matrix A − AZ∗A\nL304@P4: in Algorithm 2.1.\nL305@P4: 3.1. Truncated SVD solvers. Algorithm 3.1 is a standard method of solving a\nL306@P4-5: linear system using a truncated SVD [10]. It is based on computing the full SVD of the6 V. COPPE, D. HUYBRECHS, R. MATTHYSEN, M. WEBB ´\nL307@P5: Algorithm 3.1 Truncated SVD solver [10]\nL308@P5: Input: A ∈ C\nL309@P5: M×N , b ∈ CM, ε > 0\nL310@P5: Output: x ∈ C\nL311@P5: N such that Ax ≈ b\nL312@P5: 1: Compute the SVD, A = UΣV\nL313@P5: ∗ where\nL314@P5: UΣV\nL315@P5: ∗\nL316@P5: = ( U1 U2 ) (\nL317@P5: Σ1\nL318@P5: Σ2\nL319@P5: ) ( V1 V2 )\nL320@P5: ∗\nL321@P5: ,\nL322@P5: with 0 ≤ Σ2 < εI ≤ Σ1.\nL323@P5: 2: x ← V1Σ\nL324@P5: −1\nL325@P5: 1 U\nL326@P5: ∗\nL327@P5: 1\nL328@P5: b\nL329@P5: matrix A, and subsequently discarding the singular values smaller than a threshold\nL330@P5: ε. Assuming that M = O(N), this algorithm has cubic complexity O(N3) even when\nL331@P5: the numerical rank of A is small. For systems of low rank, the randomized algorithms\nL332@P5: that follow have a more favourable complexity. Nevertheless, because this algorithm\nL333@P5: is suggested in [2, 1], we prove bounds on the residual which are similar in flavour to\nL334@P5: those in [2, 1].\nL335@P5: Lemma 3.3. Let x be computed by Algorithm 3.1. Then\nL336@P5: ∥b − Ax∥2 ≤ inf\nL337@P5: v∈CN\nL338@P5: {∥b − Av∥2 + ε ⋅ ∥v∥2}.\nL339@P5: Proof. We substitute x = V1Σ\nL340@P5: −1\nL341@P5: 1 U\nL342@P5: ∗\nL343@P5: 1\nL344@P5: b into the residual to obtain\nL345@P5: b − Ax = (I − AV1Σ\nL346@P5: −1\nL347@P